      "label": "The integral diverges."
   },
   "worked_solution": [
      {
         "step": 1,
         "text": "The integral is improper because the upper limit of integration is infinite, so write it as \\( \\lim_{R\\to\\infty} \\int_{2}^{R} \\frac{4 x}{\\sqrt{x^{2} + 6}}\\,dx \\).",
         "point_type_id": "BC-PT-99053",
         "rule_named": "improper integral as a limit"
      },
      {
         "step": 2,
         "text": "Substitute \\( u = x^{2} + 6,\\ du = 2 x\\,dx \\). The limits become \\( u = 10 \\text{ to } u = R^{2} + 6 \\), and an antiderivative in u is \\( 4 \\sqrt{u} \\).",
         "point_type_id": "BC-PT-99003",
         "rule_named": "substitution with changed limits"
      },
      {
         "step": 3,
         "text": "Evaluate the limit: \\( \\lim_{R\\to\\infty} \\left( 4\\sqrt{R^{2} + 6} - 4 \\sqrt{10} \\right) = \\infty \\). The limit is infinite, so the integral diverges.",
         "point_type_id": "BC-PT-99004",
         "rule_named": "divergent limit"
      }
   ],
